  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1207 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nGuidance is now showing the potential for convective development \nlate in the afternoon on Monday into Tuesday evening. During the \ndaytime on Monday, we will see height rises with height falls not \narriving until the evening. Large scale ascent will still be lacking \nwith the upper level trough axis still located over the Great Basin. \nThe strongest jet level winds will be located from Baja California \nthrough Arizona all the way into the northern Plains. Additional \nwarm air advection showery activity will be possible late Monday \ninto early Tuesday with continued strong low level jet winds. \nHowever, some instability will be introduced bringing a low threat \nfor thunder.\n\nTuesday afternoon will begin to see the stronger pattern of \nincreased chances of convection. During the day on Tuesday, the \ncenter of the upper level trough will move out of the Great Basin \ninto Utah passing north of the four-corners region. Height falls \nwill occur during the day increasing large scale ascent. However, \nthe upper level jet will still be located well to the west of the \narea across the Intermountain West. Models do indicate a weak \nembedded short wave moving through the southwest flow aloft which \nmay be enough to initiate scattered convection. Low level moisture \nfrom the Gulf will surge northward in advance of the larger scale \ntrough forming a dryline like feature near the Texas/New Mexico \nstate line. Surface dew points are forecast to be in the mid to \nupper 60s by Tuesday morning mixing out into the low to mid-60s in \nthe afternoon. A very moist low level atmosphere and warm surface \ntemperatures will lead to an increase in potential instability. Mid-\nlevel lapse rates will be fairly weak with values on the order of \nonly 6 C/km due to a very moist atmosphere. Relatively low LCLs will \nbe responsible for mixed layer instabilities on the order of 1000-\n1500 J/kg. The highest potential for severe storms will be during \nthe afternoon hours with all hazards possible. Also of note will be \nthe potential of flooding from heavy rainfall. Moisture levels \nthroughout the entire atmospheric column will increase beginning as \nearly as this evening. By Tuesday, models are indicating 850mb \nmixing ratios as high as 15 g/kg which would place these values \nabove the 99th percentile. Similarly, precipitable water values are \nin the same percentile range exceeding 1.50 inches for areas east of \nthe I-27 corridor. The highest potential for heavy rainfall will \ncome late in the evening into the early morning hours on Wednesday \nas stronger large scale ascent spreads overhead. The very slow \nmovement of the trough will continue to bring lift during the day on \nWednesday further extending precipitation chances. Model guidance \ncontinues to greatly diverge beyond Wednesday guiding to a low \nconfidence forecast. A very strong front is still forecast for \nsometime late this week which could bring widespread morning \ntemperatures in the 40s next weekend.",
